%[text] ## Importing Tabular Data
%[text] Usually our data is already neatly organized in a spreadsheet. For this example, we will load data generated for a previous MHA capstone project. This data was collected to test the educational value of a remote learning module covering ultrasound. The data is stored in an excel file called "teleultrasound\_data.xlsx". 
%[text] Switch to the `MtMresources/data/unit1` folder in the Files window and look for the file. 
%[text] Before we get started, let's clear the workspace
clearvars
%%
%[text] You can directly import spreadsheets into MATLAB tables using the built-in import function. Select the "Import Data" button (found in the Home Tab)
%[text] ![](text:image:43f4)
%[text] Select the "teleultrasound\_data.xlsx" file.  
%[text] You should see the following window that has a Ribbon strip with some options already selected. Below the ribbon there is a spreadsheet viewer with the data to be imported displayed. 
%[text] ![](text:image:3de3)
%[text] 
%[text] As you can see, the table has four columns:
%[text] 1. `Student`: the student ID
%[text] 2. `Score`: The student's Likert ranking for a given question (range from 1-5)
%[text] 3. `Question`: the question ID
%[text] 4. `Site`: Location of the learning (in person or virtual) \
%[text] Notice that the [data in the table is tidy](https://salcedoe.github.io/MtMdocs/dataWrangling/tidyData/).
%[text] 1. Each row corresponds to an observation
%[text] 2. Each variable is a column
%[text] 3. Each type of observation is in a different table \
%[text] Notice that `Site` and `Question` will be imported as categorical arrays. We'll get to those in a bit. The light blue coloring in the spreadsheet view indicates what data will be imported
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DE47C496] ### Try it now
%[text] Select one of the data cells.
%[text] - What happens to the light blue coloring?
%[text] - What happens to the **Selection/History** box at the top?
%[text] - Click around. Try to select more than one cells and keep an eye on **Selection/History** \
%[text] Select all four columns and rows 2:36
%[text] - The simplest way to do this is to bring up the **Selection/History** pop-up menu and choose the (Initial) setting.
%[text] - Make sure that the range reads "A2:D36" and **Variable Names Row =** `1`. \
%[text] Although Column A, Student, has numbers in it, these numbers do not represent a value, they represent a label (student 1, student 2, student 3). So, instead of loading this data as numbers we should load it as Categorical. So:
%[text] - Set Column A to "Categorical" \
%[text] The Name box determines the name of the variable. Instead of a long variable name, I usually prefer a short one for Tables, so let's rename the variable `T`
%[text] - In the **Name** box, rename `teleultrasound_data` to `T`. This will set the created variable name to `T`. \
%[text] To import the data, click on the Green "Import Selection" button.
%[text] - What happens? Hint: Look in the workspace. \
%[text] Look in the workspace. You should have a variable `T`. If, instead, you have a variable `teleultrasound_data`, simply rename the variable in the workspace by double-clicking on the name in the workspace.
%%
%[text] ### Table Properties
%[text] Review the Table properties

%%
%[text] ### Quick Analysis
%[text] Now that we have imported the table, we can perform some quick data analysis on the data.
%[text] The function `summary` gives us a quick summary of the different columns
summary(T)
%[text] - Notice that we get different summaries for the different data types.
%[text] - For Numeric classes, we get typical stats, like Min, Median, Max
%[text] - For Categorical classes, we get counts of the different categories \
%%
%[text] ### Basic stats
%[text] We can perform basic stats on the data by simply plugging in a column of data into one of our MATLAB stats functions.
mean(T.Score)
std(T.Score)
%[text] - This returns the overall mean and standard deviation of the `Score` column
%[text] - Does it make sense to calculate an overall goal like this?  \

